Using `List[str]` instead raises `AttributeError` in the same way.

Here's a slightly smaller example which throws the exception.
```python
from abc import ABC
from abc import abstractmethod
from typing import Any


class Test(ABC):

    @property
    def foo(self) -> Any:
        """Getter"""

    @foo.setter  # type: ignore
    @abstractmethod
    def foo(self: Any) -> None:
        """Setter"""
```
What I'm mostly interested here is:
- what is the correct way to annotate abstract properties and is that currently expected to work?
- does the example expose a bug in mypy?

I tried with the current `master` branch and it has the same behavior.

@dokai 
> does the example expose a bug in mypy?

Yes, practically every crash is a bug. (In some sense it is even more than bug, this is probably why label ``crash`` added to this issue has strong red color.)

@dokai 
> what is the correct way to annotate abstract properties and is that currently expected to work?

From Python point of view the _current_ way is just to use two decorators, see https://docs.python.org/3/library/abc.html#abc.abstractproperty but I am not sure mypy supports this.
